# State lives in one S3 bucket shared across every project generated from
# this template — not a bucket this project owns. The bucket is created once,
# outside this template, in the account-level bootstrap; see STANDARDS.md's
# "Hosting". Isolation between projects sharing it is IAM, not bucket
# topology: this project's deploy role is scoped to its own
# `{{ project_slug }}/*` prefix and nothing wider.
#
# Backend blocks cannot interpolate: no `var.`, no locals, no `${}`. That
# used to mean the bucket name was a Copier literal because it was this
# project's own bucket; now bucket AND region are both constants every
# project shares, not per-project answers — `region` here is deliberately
# NOT `{{ aws_region }}` (that variable is this project's own Lambda/resource
# region, in variables.tf, and can differ from project to project). The
# bucket lives in one fixed place, set once by whoever ran the account
# bootstrap, regardless of where any given project deploys its resources —
# reusing `aws_region` here would silently point the backend at the wrong
# region for any project that picks a non-default one. `key` is the one
# thing that legitimately varies — same project, different environment — so
# it's the one piece a literal can't hold. It's omitted here (a partial
# backend configuration) and supplied per apply:
#
#   terraform init -backend-config="key={{ project_slug }}/<environment>/terraform.tfstate"
#
# `use_lockfile` is S3-native state locking, per key — no DynamoDB lock table,
# and no contention between projects or environments sharing the bucket. It
# needs Terraform >= 1.10, which main.tf's `required_version` pins.
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket       = "natedogg12501-workflow-tfstate"
    region       = "us-east-1"
    encrypt      = true
    use_lockfile = true
  }
}
